# T7-006 -- more layers than planes
#
# *** This scenario does NOT diff clean, and is not part of the clean set. ***
# It is drm-cxx#240: on a driver whose planes have no settable zpos, the
# composition canvas is placed below the layers it carries. Both sides get it
# wrong and they get it wrong differently -- upstream puts the canvas on
# plane[1], under all eight overlays; the port puts it on plane[8], above
# l1..l7 but still under l8 on plane[9]. One plane too low. The scenario is
# kept because it is the reproduction, and it is what will show the fix when
# upstream decides. run.sh will report it dirty until then.
#
# Not a corner of the fleet: 773 of 1001 atomic devices in the drmdb corpus
# (77%) have no plane whose zpos can be written -- i915 358, amdgpu 211,
# nvidia-drm 90. On every one of them plane index *is* the stacking order, so
# which plane the canvas lands on is the only lever there is. Posted to #240
# along with the side-by-side above.
#
# vkms offers nine usable planes on its CRTC. Twelve layers is three more
# than can be scanned out directly, so the allocator places what it can and
# the rest have to reach the screen some other way: blended on the CPU into
# a full-screen canvas that takes a plane of its own.
#
# Two implementations can agree on every layer they place and still disagree
# completely here. The choices are all invisible to the placement itself:
# which plane carries the canvas, where it sits in the stack, whether the
# planes the overflow would have used are cleared, and whether the canvas is
# armed in the same atomic request as everything else -- which it must be,
# because a kernel that refuses it refuses the whole frame, every placed
# layer included.
#
# It is also the newest code in the port, and the only scenario so far whose
# subject is a code path the allocator never reaches.
#
# Frames one and two configure and then settle. The `del` drops the scene
# back under the plane count, so the canvas should disappear entirely and
# every surviving layer go back to a plane of its own -- the return path
# matters as much as the overflow, and a canvas left armed after it is no
# longer needed costs a plane and a full-screen blend per frame forever.

add bg  0 0 0 0 0
add l1  1 0 0 64 64
add l2  2 64 0 64 64
add l3  3 128 0 64 64
add l4  4 192 0 64 64
add l5  5 256 0 64 64
add l6  6 320 0 64 64
add l7  7 384 0 64 64
add l8  8 448 0 64 64
add l9  9 512 0 64 64
add l10 10 576 0 64 64
add l11 11 640 0 64 64

frame
frame

# Back under the plane count: the canvas should go away.
del l11
del l10
del l9
del l8
frame
frame
